$DIR/main.p:21:1: onPanic is the panic handler, so it must never return
    A panic stops the program: it does not unwind, so there is nothing for the
    handler to return to. Every path through it must end in an endless loop, or
    in a call of a function that never returns, such as os.Exit, or a function
    whose every path ends the same way. A call through a func value or an
    interface, and recursion, count as returning.
    onPanic can return here (main.p:21).
    A kernel has no operating system to fall back on when it panics, so
    Plutonium makes the program choose what happens at compile time, and checks
    the choice: the runtime calls the handler directly, with nothing to
    register, so it works from the first instruction.
    End it by halting:
        for {
            x86.Cli()
            x86.Hlt()
        }
    (see "Panics in freestanding programs" in the Plutonium spec, section 17.4)
    (run "pluto explain E2583" for a longer explanation)
